This post is an extension of the one about the nonfarm payroll. BLS publishes two employment numbers: the
household survey counts jobs based on headcounts, while the payroll survey counts the number of jobs (and could artificially inflate the number). The two measures disagree by millions. The goal of the post is to separate the part of the gap that is simply due to definitions from the residuals and show when and why the residual moves.

## Comparison between CPS vs. CES

| | Household survey (CPS) | Payroll survey (CES) |
|---|---|---|
| Unit | people | jobs |
| Object | about 60,000 households | businesses and government agencies |
| Self-employed, unpaid family, private-household workers | counted | not counted |
| Farm workers | counted | not counted |
| On unpaid leave | counted as employed | not counted |
| A person with two jobs | counted once | counted multiple times |

This comparison table shows the difference between the measurement of CES and CPS data. All data points can be relatively easily bridged except the last one. CES only cares about the total number of jobs and does not make a distinction when the same person has multiple jobs at the same time. Thus, precisely bridging CES and CPS would be troublesome. However, since the majority of the multiple jobholders (90.5%) have exactly two jobs, it wouldn't be a stretch to assume 2 as the average. 

$$
\text{CPS Adjusted} = \text{Household Employment} -
                      \text{Agriculture} -
                      \text{Unpaid Absent}
$$
BLS has a technical note on bridging CES and CPS employment data, and the equation is shown above. 
(bls.gov/web/empsit/ces_cps_trends.htm).--cite this

## Data and weights

[WRITE - IPUMS CPS basic monthly microdata, January 2019 to August 2026,
91 months. No October 2025: the household survey was not collected during
the government shutdown. 
Payrolls: CES total nonfarm, not seasonally
adjusted (FRED PAYNSA), current vintage as of 28 September 2026.]

[WRITE - weights, in two or three sentences. Every number in this post is a
population total, so each respondent counts for the number of people they
represent. With COMPWT, the composite weight BLS uses for its published
estimates, January 2019 employment comes out at 154.964 million, which is
BLS's published figure (LNU02000000) exactly. The basic weight WTFINL gives
155.580 million, 616,000 too many.]

```{r}
#| eval: false
#| code-fold: true
#| code-summary: "The weighted totals behind every figure"
employment <- cps |>
  filter(EMPSTAT %in% c(10, 12),
         AGE >= 16) |>
  mutate(ag = IND >= 170 & IND <= 290) |>
  group_by(YEAR, MONTH) |>
  summarise(
    employed                  = sum(COMPWT) / 1e6,
    agriculture               = sum(COMPWT[ag]) / 1e6,
    self_employed             = sum(COMPWT[CLASSWKR == 13 & !ag]) / 1e6,
    unpaid_family_workers     = sum(COMPWT[CLASSWKR == 29 & !ag]) / 1e6,
    private_household_workers = sum(COMPWT[IND == 9290 & !(CLASSWKR %in% c(13, 29))]) / 1e6,
    multiple_jobholders       = sum(COMPWT[MULTJOB == 2 & !ag & !(CLASSWKR %in% c(13, 29))]) / 1e6,
    absent_ws                 = sum(COMPWT[EMPSTAT == 12 & !ag & !(CLASSWKR %in% c(13, 29))]) / 1e6,
    .groups = "drop"
  )
```

## The gap

![](fig1_levels_gap.png)

[WRITE - read the figure. The gap was about 7 million in 2019 and narrowed
to 2-4 million by late 2024. Both series are unadjusted, so the gap has a
seasonal wobble. Point at the single-month jump from about 2 to about 6
million between December 2024 and January 2025; it gets explained below.]

## What the definitions explain

![](fig2_decomposition_by_year.png)

[WRITE - read the figure. Bars above zero widen the gap, bars below narrow
it; the dots are the net gap. Self-employment is the largest piece; multiple
jobholders pull the other way. Unpaid family workers are about 0.04 million,
so they share a slice with private-household workers.]

[WRITE - the residual across years: about 2.2 million in 2019, under 1
million in 2020-23, negative in 2024, about 1.5 million in 2025. Then the
mechanism: each January the household survey switches to new population
estimates and never revises earlier months. January 2025's update added 2.0
million to employment, mostly from higher immigration estimates (BLS). Your
own check: household employment normally falls about 0.83 million from
December to January (2020-24 average), but rose 1.05 million in January
2025, an excess of about 1.9 million. So during 2024 the household survey
was undercounting; the residual being negative is consistent with that.]

## When the residual moves

![](fig3_residual_monthly.png)

[WRITE - the April 2020 result. Absent wage and salary workers jumped from
3.5 million (February) to 9.6 million (April). If unpaid leave drove the
residual it should have risen; it fell from +3.0 to -2.2 million. So in
spring 2020 something else dominated. Candidates, stated as untested: the
payroll survey counts anyone paid for any part of the pay period including
the 12th, so fast layoffs still show up as jobs; and household interviewing
was disrupted, with response rates falling.]

[WRITE - January 2026, if you include it: household employment fell 2.05
million December to January, against a normal 0.83, suggesting an update
of roughly -1.2 million. CHECK BLS's 2026 population-control note first.]

## Takeaway

[WRITE - what the reader should now know: most of the gap is definitions;
the residual is small in normal years, and when it moves there is usually a
nameable measurement reason, which alternates between the two surveys.
Which number to trust depends on the month.]

## Caveats

[WRITE - unpaid leave cannot be measured in the monthly files (it is only
asked in the March supplement), so it stays in the residual. Multiple
jobholders are counted as one extra job each; 90.5% hold exactly two, so
this slightly understates payroll jobs. Annual 2026 covers only
January-August, so its seasonal pattern does not cancel. Payroll numbers
from April 2025 on will be re-benchmarked next February.]

## Data and code

- **Household survey:** IPUMS CPS basic monthly samples, extract `cps_00003`.
  Sarah Flood, Miriam King, Renae Rodgers, Steven Ruggles, J. Robert Warren,
  Daniel Backman, Etienne Breton, Grace Cooper, Julia A. Rivera Drew, Stephanie
  Richards, David Van Riper, and Kari C.W. Williams. *IPUMS CPS: Version 13.0*
  [dataset]. Minneapolis, MN: IPUMS, 2025. <https://doi.org/10.18128/D030.V13.0>
- **Payroll survey:** BLS Current Employment Statistics, total nonfarm, not
  seasonally adjusted, via FRED (`PAYNSA`), accessed 28 September 2026.
- **Bridge method:** BLS, [Employment from the CPS adjusted to the CES concept](https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/ces_cps_trends.htm).
- **Population controls:** BLS, [Population control adjustments, January 2025](https://www.bls.gov/cps/methods/population-controls/population-control-adjustments-2025.pdf).
- **Code:** [github.com/1iiiiii/Data-Visualization-in-R](https://github.com/1iiiiii/Data-Visualization-in-R).
  The raw IPUMS extract is not redistributed; the README explains how to request it.
